Neapolitan Pizzeria & Birreria in Laguna Beach and Fuoco Pizzeria Napoletana in Fullerton are going for lovers of authentic Neapolitan pizzas. Both full-service locations, which opened recently, are outfitted with wood-fired ovens imported from Naples.
Other invaders include build-your-own concepts – Blaze Pizza (Irvine and Pasadena) and Pieology (Irvine and Fullerton). Pieology plans to open 20 fast-casual restaurants over the next year. Blaze Pizza, launched by the founders of Wetzel's Pretzels, is also planning major growth in multiple states.
Most restaurant chains scouting Orange County seek destination malls with high-volume foot traffic. Think Fashion Island, Irvine Spectrum Center or South Coast Plaza. But that's not the strategy of Pitfire Artisan Pizza.
"I don't want you to come here because you also happen to be buying tennis shoes," Pitfire founder Paul Hibler said of mall-based restaurants.
The free-spirited Hibler would rather be defined by what he's creating. His modern-day pizza parlors, founded 15 years ago, have become gathering spots for families and friends to unplug while indulging on rustic, fire-singed pizzas.
"My mission is to create a community place because we're losing them in our lives," he said.
Los Angeles-based Pitfire Pizza is opening its first Orange County location Thursday in an old Marie Callender's on 17th Street in Costa Mesa. Building on the area's local history, the remodeled 5,000-square-foot restaurant embodies the area's surf and skateboard culture. Red, orange and yellow striped walls pay tribute to the iconic Ocean Pacific surf brand. Glossy clear countertops mimic the waxy finish of a surfboard. Booth seats are furnished with the same upholstery found in a Volkswagen bus.
The stand-alone restaurant is near other craft food and beverage retailers – Kean Coffee, Avanti Café, Hi-Time Wine Cellars, French's Cupcake Bakery, SusieCakes.
"This is the perfect place for me to plant my flag in Orange County," Hibler said.
Pitfire's local debut comes as a handful of other thin-crust pizza concepts establish locations across the county. Two weeks ago, Pieology, whose key investor is former tennis star Michael Chang, opened its second build-your-own pizza concept at the Irvine Spectrum Center.
Pieology founder Carl Chang said the "pizza wars" are definitely on.
"But within our category, we found a special niche," said Chang, Michael Chang's older brother.
Hibler also believes he's establishing a role for his bohemian concept, which is growing at a slower rate than his competitors.
The 17th Street location is Pitfire's sixth restaurant to open since 1997. A few more are planned in Orange County, but no leases have been signed, Hibler said.
In January, a seventh Pitfire will open in Manhattan Beach.
"I don't want to see a Pitfire at every corner," said Hibler, who ran a catering business before launching Pitfire.
Though the Costa Mesa Pitfire looks different than others, two things are the same: the seasonally influenced menu and community atmosphere.
"We're taking off where the pizza parlors of your youth left off," Hibler said.
Favorites such as the Burrata and Green, Eggs and Ham pizzas are served in a large format restaurant that blends modern conveniences with old-school charm. Servers use hand-held devices to process orders and checks. Diners sit in roomy booths or communal tables as a deejay spins vinyl records.
The pizza dough is made in-house daily and rests for two days before baking. The fermentation process produces a caramelized crust after a four-minute baking period. Toppings are sourced locally, with a bulk of the produce coming from the Santa Monica Farmers Market.
Lingering is encouraged.
Though you order at a counter, Hibler said service doesn't stop there. Additional orders for food, beer and cocktails can be made at the table with servers equipped with mobile devices.
Hibler said he knows he's doing something right when he sees family, friends and strangers engaging in lively conversations – no cellphones in sight.
